==Post-match and aftermath==
Tottenham Hotspur won their first trophy since winning the [[2007–08 Football League Cup]], and the first European trophy since prevailing in the [[1983–84 UEFA Cup]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cz9yney9391t|title=Tottenham 1-0 Man Utd: Brennan Johnson goal seals Europa League win for Spurs &amp; ends 17-year wait for trophy |first=Simon |last=Stone|author-link=Simon Stone|work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation|access-date=22 May 2025|date=21 May 2025}}</ref> Tottenham head coach [[Ange Postecoglou]] became the first Australian and the first manager from a non-[[CONMEBOL]] and [[UEFA]] member association country to [[List of UEFA club competition winning managers|win a European trophy]]. Having won the [[1999 OFC Club Championship]] with [[South Melbourne FC|South Melbourne]], he became the first manager to win continental club titles in Europe and outside Europe since [[Marcello Lippi]], who won the [[1995–96 UEFA Champions League]] and [[2013 AFC Champions League]]. Tottenham and South Korean national team captain [[Son Heung-min]] became the first European competition-winning captain to hail from the [[Asia–Pacific]]. Postecoglou claimed the victory for his team  showed he was a serial winner and could transform the North London club.<ref>{{cite news |title=Tottenham win the Europa League: Ange Postecoglou says first trophy in 17 years will help to change club |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11675/13372221/tottenham-win-the-europa-league-ange-postecoglou-says-first-trophy-in-17-years-will-help-to-change-club |work=[[Sky Sports]] |access-date=5 June 2025 |date=22 May 2025}}</ref> Despite winning Tottenham their first major trophy in 17 years, Postecoglou was sacked by the North London club on 6 June 2025 with the club citing poor domestic league form  as the main  reason for his dismissal.<ref>{{cite news |date=6 June 2025 |title=Club statement - Ange Postecoglou Departs |url=https://www.tottenhamhotspur.com/news/2025/june/club-statement-ange-postecoglou-departs/ |access-date=6 June 2025 |publisher=Tottenham Hotspur|work=TottenhamHotspur.com}}</ref> With Tottenham having finished 17th in the [[2024–25 Premier League]], one spot above relegation, their Europa League win also made them the European club competition winners with the lowest-ever league finish. The record had previously been held by [[Inter Milan]], who won the [[1993–94 UEFA Cup]], while being placed 13th in the [[1993–94 Serie A]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 May 2025|title=La finale di Europa League tra due squadre quasi disperate |url=https://www.ilpost.it/2025/05/21/finale-europa-league-tottenham-manchester-united-crisi-champions/ |access-date=11 June 2025 |website=Il Post |language=it}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=21 May 2025 |title=Il trionfo in Europa League e non solo: il Tottenham vola in Champions da 17° in Premier League |url=https://www.goal.com/it/liste/il-trionfo-in-europa-league-e-non-solo-il-tottenham-vola-in-champions-da-17-in-premier-league/blt0a01792207447c30 |access-date=11 June 2025 |website=Goal.com Italia|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Inter-Calcio.it : Archivio Storico Campionato 1993-1994 |url=https://www.inter-calcio.it/archivio_storico_campionato1993_1994.html |access-date=11 June 2025|website=inter-calcio.it}}</ref>

This was the second time that Manchester United ended a major European final without scoring (2–0 loss to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in the [[2009 UEFA Champions League final]]), and for only the third time in a European title-deciding match (also 1–0 loss to [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] in the [[1999 UEFA Super Cup]]). With this defeat, United failed to qualify for the European Cup/UEFA Champions League in two consecutive seasons for the first time since 1992, and for any European competition for only the second time since 1990 (also in [[2014–15 Manchester United F.C. season|2014–15]]). This was also the first time United lost four times against Tottenham in the same season. They lost five in the past six meetings between the sides, and United's latest win against Tottenham was in October 2022 as of this final. They also had failed to defeat Tottenham outside of their home at [[Old Trafford]] since the 3–0 victory in October 2021.<ref name="11v11"/> Furthermore, United had failed to defeat or eliminate a fellow English side in Europe since beating [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] 1–0 and 2–1 (3–1 on aggregate) in the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Quarter-finals|2010–11 UEFA Champions League quarter-finals]]. This also meant that since winning the [[UEFA Champions League]] in [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]], United had now lost four of the latest five major European finals they had contested, having lost none of their first four from [[1968 European Cup final|1968]] to 2008; their only European final victory from 2009 to 2025 was the [[2017 UEFA Europa League final]]. Despite the defeat, United head coach [[Ruben Amorim]] insisted his side were the better team throughout the match.<ref>{{cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |title=Belief in Amorim remains at Man Utd - but for how long?|work=[[BBC Sport]]|publisher= BBC|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c23mgk1xel4o |access-date=5 June 2025 |date=22 May 2025}}</ref>

